  "text": "A modest collection from a western city claims to represent the verse of a particular cultural group. The question is how to tell that verse from the more general English tradition. The promotional note points to a Celtic‑like attentiveness to word and image, but that description is vague and could apply anywhere. Such attentiveness, which any poet needs, is shaped either by the language and history of the poet’s community or by the current concerns of that community. As an illustration of the first, one early‑20th‑century poet whose imagery and diction echo the older local poetic tradition would fit, while a later poet whose technical style resembles mainstream English poetry yet is deeply preoccupied with the region’s landscape and spirit exemplifies the second. In this anthology some pieces belong to the first group, a few to the second, but most could have been written by English writers living in England. There are indeed some fine English‑style poems, and a companion volume from the same press contains more of the distinctive regional work, which are the strongest selections."
